  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-28T07:51:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "a3e606f9-42ef-4602-aab3-a5e2f12a6c87",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 280751\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1251 AM PDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry and seasonable conditions continue through Thursday.\n\n* Precipitation chances and cooler temperatures return to the\n  region late this week and continue through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nExpect a couple more days of pleasant weather before the next system \nmoves in towards the end of the week. Zonal flow aloft continues to \nmaintain partly to mostly cloudy skies and temperatures within a few \ndegrees of normal for late October. This means highs in the upper \n70s for Las Vegas, the low 80s in the Colorado River Valley, and the \nupper 60s to low 70s in Owens Valley. \n\nAn incoming system will bring precipitation, gusty winds, and cooler \ntemperatures to the forecast area in a few days. A trough will \nsettle off the West Coast starting Thursday, a favorable position to \ndraw moisture from Tropical Storm Vance up the Gulf of California \nand into the Desert Southwest. PWATs rise to over 200 percent of \nnormal for this time of year. This setup results in precipitation \nchances across northwestern Arizona, parts of southern Nevada, and \neastern California near the Colorado River as early as Thursday \nnight, lasting into Saturday. Heightened instability with CAPE up to \n500 J/kg may lead to particularly efficient rain rates in southern \nMohave County on Friday afternoon. However, more widespread \nprecipitation potential arrives late Saturday through Sunday as the \ntrough moves inland, splitting as it tracks east into one low over \nthe Pacific Northwest and another over Southern California. The \nsouthern feature will draw subtropical moisture into the region, but \ntrajectory of the moisture will be a major factor in which areas \nreceive precipitation. Southwesterly flow and rain shadowing behind \nthe San Bernardino Mountains could greatly reduce rainfall for \nsections of the Mojave Desert. Areas further to the east seem to \nhave better precipitation chances. Amplitude of the trough itself \nand the presence of embedded shortwaves could also affect storm \ntotals. Cold air advection as the trough moves in will send snow \nlevels to around 8500 feet, meaning the higher peaks could receive a \nfew inches of snow. Gusty northerly winds pick up on Sunday and \nMonday as the system exits, especially in the Colorado River Valley. \nTemperatures will be around 10 degrees below normal over the weekend \nand early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nand diurnal winds expected through the TAF period, with afternoon \nbreezes generally around 8 kts. Can't rule out an occasional gust to \n15 kts in the afternoon, with southeast being the favored direction. \nSCT-BKN aoa 25 kft increase through the overnight period and will \ncontinue through Wednesday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds will be generally \nlight and diurnal through the TAF period, with occasional gusts of \n15 to 20 kts from the south-southeast at the Las Vegas Valley, \nColorado River Valley, and Owens Valley TAF sites. KDAG will remain \ngenerally light from the east through the day on Wednesday. SCT-BKN \naoa 25 kft will increase through the overnight period and will \ncontinue through Wednesday.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
